==== 3.5 Visual Diagnostics: Scatter and Funnel Plots ====

We generate a '''scatter plot''' and a '''funnel plot''' for '''every''' exposure–outcome
association before adjudicating the model in [[#5. Decision Matrix: Which Results to Report|Section 5]].
They reveal outlier and pleiotropic instruments that the summary statistics (Cochran's ''Q'',
the MR-Egger intercept) can under-flag, and they frequently drive the model choice on inspection
even when the numeric trigger is borderline. Record the model used '''and the reason''' for each
association.

'''Scatter plot.''' Each point is an instrument: SNP→exposure effect on ''x'', SNP→outcome effect
on ''y''; the overlaid lines are the method estimates (IVW, MR-Egger, weighted median).
* Points should scatter around a single line through the origin.
* A high-leverage point sitting off the IVW line (e.g. the ''APOE'' region for lipid exposures) is a candidate outlier / pleiotropic SNP.
* If the MR-Egger or weighted-median slope diverges from IVW, suspect pleiotropy.

<syntaxhighlight lang="r">
res <- mr(dat, method_list = c("mr_ivw_mre", "mr_egger_regression", "mr_weighted_median"))
mr_scatter_plot(res, dat)[[1]]
</syntaxhighlight>

'''Funnel plot.''' Per-SNP Wald ratio on ''x'' against instrument precision (1/SE) on ''y''.
* A '''symmetric''' inverted funnel around the pooled estimate ⇒ balanced pleiotropy; IVW valid.
* '''Asymmetry''' ⇒ directional pleiotropy ⇒ MR-Egger or weighted mode.
* A point far from the body of the funnel is the same outlier the scatter shows and a candidate for removal (MR-PRESSO).

<syntaxhighlight lang="r">
ss <- mr_singlesnp(dat)
mr_funnel_plot(ss)[[1]]
</syntaxhighlight>

How the plots map onto the model choice is given in the [[#5. Decision Matrix: Which Results to Report|Decision Matrix (Section 5)]]; the plots can trigger a model (e.g. MR-PRESSO) on visual grounds even when the numeric rule is ambiguous.

'''Worked example — LDL-C → Alzheimer's disease.''' The naive IVW estimate was a large positive
effect (β ≈ +0.58 per SD LDL-C; OR ≈ 1.8), biologically implausible. The scatter showed a
high-leverage ''APOE''-region cluster well off the IVW slope, and the funnel showed the matching
outlier and asymmetry. We selected '''MR-PRESSO''' on that visual basis; it removed the ''APOE''
outliers and the estimate attenuated to ≈ +0.03 (null). The naive positive was ''APOE''
horizontal pleiotropy (''APOE'' acts on AD through amyloid, not through LDL), not an LDL causal
effect. Cochran's ''Q'' is significant here too, but the plots identify the specific offending
locus and are what triggered the decision.


